What the report tells you (Excel, plus a markdown summary). In my test, with 4 scripts on 2 servers, it found all of these:

DUPLICATE: identical scripts. Keep one copy.
PARAMETERIZE: same logic with different values, and it lists the values. For example App1 | 30 vs App2 | 14 shows that two log-cleanup scripts could become one script with -Path and -Days parameters.
SIMILAR: overlapping logic worth merging.
Shared functions: helpers like Write-Log copied into many scripts, which belong in one .psm1 module. It also catches copies that have drifted apart.
Same script on many servers: run it once from a central server with Invoke-Command -ComputerName instead of scheduling it everywhere.
Schedule overlap: tasks firing at the same time or within 15 minutes of each other, flagged as a strong merge candidate when the scripts are also similar.
Security flags: hardcoded passwords and plaintext SecureString values. Passwords are masked in the report.
Inventory: cmdlets, modules, target servers, UNC paths, output files and email usage per script.

The analyzer handles the mechanical comparison. Once it shows which scripts belong together, CONSOLIDATION_REPORT.md plus those scripts with server names and passwords scrubbed is a good thing to bring to me to draft the merged, parameterized version.
